## Summary

The pnpm side of publishing platform variants, part 1 of 3 ([platform-variants RFC](https://github.com/pnpm/rfcs/blob/main/pnpr/text/0000-platform-variants.md#publishing-with-pnpm) with pnpm/rfcs#70, Implementation step 5). Follows pnpm/pnpm#17648 (merged), for the shared 128-byte tag limit.

**`publishConfig.variants`** is an array with one entry per archive:

```json
"publishConfig": {
  "variants": [
    { "targets": ["any"], "files": ["lib/**"] },
    {
      "targets": ["linux-x64", "linux-x64-musl"],
      "files": ["native/linux-x64/example-cli"],
      "manifest": { "bin": { "example-cli": "native/linux-x64/example-cli" } }
    }
  ]
}
```

A platform variant's tag is its targets sorted by bytes and joined with `+` (`linux-x64+linux-x64-musl`). The `any` entry is the version's archive for every other host. A declaration without one publishes a version with no `any` archive.

**Checks before anything is packed** (`VariantDeclaration::read`):

- Each target is `any` or a canonical platform tag. When a close spelling exists, the error gives it: `linux-x64-gnu` → `linux-x64`, `linux-x64-napi8-musl` → `linux-x64-musl-napi8`. `canonical_spelling` lives in `pnpm-platform-variants`, shared with pnpr.
- No target appears twice, within an entry or across entries, and `any` stands alone. Targets are canonical tags, so this also rules out ties: two variants can only tie on a host through two platforms of equal rank that one host matches, and those are the same platfor
