## Summary

A rerun of `pnpm publish` that signs provenance again sends a new statement, so pnpr treats it as a new request and answers 409, already published. pnpm now confirms the published version against what the rerun would have published, as the [platform variants RFC](https://github.com/pnpm/rfcs/pull/70) specifies. It reports the version as published only when all of these hold:

- the version's release record (`<name>/<version>+r0`) has the same set of variant tags, and names the same `.tgz` as the input of the `any` archive and of each variant;
- when this run signs provenance, `/-/npm/v1/attestations/<name>@<version>` has a statement whose subjects are exactly the archives this run signs for (PURL and SHA-512);
- the dist-tag the publish sets points at the version.

A version without an `any` archive has no primary input in its record, so it is compared by its variants only. In every other case the publish fails with `ERR_PNPM_PUBLISHED_RELEASE_DIFFERS`, naming the archive, the missing statement, or the dist-tag. The check runs only for a non-staged publish that gets a 409 from a registry declared `serverType: pnpr`.

